INT. ICE CAVE - DAY

Blue walls. A low ceiling of glacier. Meltwater drips into a black pool.

HANNELORE KAST, 50s, expedition leader, frostbitten nose, sits by a dying camp stove. She wears three jackets.

BJORN STAAL, 30s, burly, one arm in a makeshift sling, leans on the wall beside her.

OTIENO MWANGI, 20s, climber, sleeps with his head on his pack.

PRIYA RAMAKRISHNAN, 40s, doctor, kneels over a med kit. She counts bandages.

TOMASZ WIERZBA, 60s, the oldest of them, grey beard crusted with ice, stares at the cave mouth.

HANNELORE
Weather window?

TOMASZ
Closing. The wind has turned.

HANNELORE
How long?

TOMASZ
Two hours. Maybe three.

BJORN
Enough to walk out?

TOMASZ
Enough to walk out if you are young and have two arms.

BJORN
I have one and a half.

PRIYA
You have one and a fracture. Stay put.

HANNELORE
We stay together. We always said that.

TOMASZ
We said many things in the base camp.

HANNELORE
Tomasz.

TOMASZ
I am old, Hanne. I have seen this mountain take better people than us. But it does not like to be stared at. If we leave now, it lets us go.

PRIYA
And Otieno?

TOMASZ
He can walk. Wake him gently.

BJORN
What about the sled?

PRIYA
I will pull the sled.

BJORN
You're half my size.

PRIYA
I am twice your sense.

HANNELORE
Enough. Pack. Everything useful, nothing sentimental.

A distant THRUM. Rotors.

They all look up.
